@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+# Owner snapshot prototype evidence
+
+Status: test-only experiment in an isolated worktree based on
+`4607249e430dac1c961df4dc615beae88e33cec8`. It is compiled only for
+`codex-thread-store` tests. It exposes no app-server API and has not been used
+with an installed Codex application or a personal profile.
+
+The unchanged read path red fixture in the Orbit repository, commit
+`bad85521f9dc6adf04a580e0dc8239b82f6c6f3b`, observed a third owner turn
+appearing on page two after page one had already returned a cursor. This
+prototype's new test cannot be run on the unchanged source because its
+snapshot API did not exist there. The red observation is evidence of the
+cursor problem, not proof of a complete owner protocol.
+
+`local/live_writer.rs` takes a test-only owner barrier across durable JSONL
+write, history projection, and rollout path sync. The barrier combines a
+shared in-process mutex with a Codex-home file lock. Snapshot open takes the
+same barrier, starts one read transaction on each SQLite database, executes
+the metadata and checkpoint `SELECT` statements before reading the rollout,
+validates a complete standalone JSONL prefix against the history checkpoint,
+and materializes metadata, turns, and item JSON into one generation. Later
+cursor pages read only that generation. The generation retains the selected
+rollout prefix bytes.
+
+The focused test used a temporary Codex home, reconciled its synthetic thread
+metadata into state SQLite, and appended turns through the real
+`LocalThreadStore` write path. It verified that a third append waits while the
+barrier is held, a two-turn generation returns only turn two on its second
+page after that append, a fresh generation returns all three turns, a cursor
+from the old generation is rejected on the new one, and the new JSONL prefix
+extends the captured prefix.
+
+Measured command, inside Orbit's shared resource slice:
+
+```sh
+CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/var/tmp/orbit-codex-owner-snapshot-target CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 \
+bun run scripts/limited.ts /usr/bin/timeout 120s /usr/bin/cargo test \
+  --manifest-path /var/tmp/orbit-codex-owner-snapshot-proto/codex-rs/Cargo.toml \
+  -p codex-thread-store owner_snapshot_prototype_keeps_cursor_pages_on_one_generation \
+  -- --nocapture
+```
+
+Final result: 1 passed, 0 failed, 256 filtered out. The first run compiled
+but failed because the synthetic fixture had no state row. The fixture was
+corrected using the repository's existing rollout reconciliation path, then
+the focused test passed twice. All Cargo and test child processes exited.
+
+This does not establish production coherence. The barrier is compiled only in
+tests. Most metadata changes, archive, revert, fork, deletion, compression,
+and unpatched writers do not participate. There is no crash journal, recovery
+protocol, token expiry, resource quota, owner restart behavior, or independent
+cross-process test. The unsupported rollout shapes are rejected by the
+snapshot opener but their branches have not been individually tested. An
+unpatched writer can bypass the lock. The personal attachment path must stay
+disabled.
+
+## State-only title writer extension
+
+One previously excluded writer path now participates in the test-only barrier:
+`update_thread_metadata` when its patch has a title and does not require a
+rollout compatibility write. This path changes state SQLite. A combined patch
+that may call the live writer does not take this added lock because doing so
+would need a different lock order.
+
+The focused test was extended before changing the writer. With the owner
+barrier held, the unchanged title path completed within 50 ms, and the test
+failed at the intended assertion. The red command above exited 101, 0 passed,
+1 failed. After the writer patch, the same command exited 0, 1 passed,
+0 failed. The new test also verifies that the old captured generation keeps
+its title and a fresh generation reads the updated title. No personal profile
+was used. This adds one measured metadata writer path, not general metadata
+coverage.
+
+## Thread section move extension
+
+`move_thread_to_section` is a separate state SQLite writer path. The snapshot
+now captures the section ID, position, and entered time from its pinned state
+read view. The test was extended before modifying this writer. While the
+owner barrier was held, the unchanged section move completed within 50 ms.
+The red run exited 101 at that exact wait assertion, 0 passed, 1 failed.
+
+The test-only section move now takes the same owner barrier before its state
+mutation. The identical focused command then exited 0, 1 passed, 0 failed.
+The old generation remained outside a section. A fresh generation contained
+the built-in pinned section ID, a position, and an entered time. This proves
+coordination for this one section move in the synthetic fixture. It does not
+cover section creation, deletion, reordering across threads, or an unpatched
+process. The app-server and personal attachment paths remain unchanged.
+
+## Archive extension
+
+`archive_threads` moves the rollout to the archived collection and then updates
+state SQLite. The focused test now shuts down its live writer, holds the owner
+barrier, and starts a real archive. Before adding the barrier to the archive
+path, the archive completed during the held generation. The test failed at
+the intended 50 ms wait assertion, exit 101, 0 passed, 1 failed. After adding
+the test-only barrier after the archive writer locks and before the rollout
+scan, the same command exited 0, 1 passed, 0 failed. The captured generation
+remained pageable after archive and a new active snapshot was rejected.
+
+This proves coordination for one synthetic active paginated thread in one
+patched test process. Unarchive, archive descendants, cross-process unpatched
+writers, crash between file move and state update, and production behavior are
+not measured. No installed application or personal profile was used.
